# until the run was stopped. Neither says anything about the work in flight or
# about the next request, so cooling down and refilling is the right response.
SOFT_STOP_REASONS = frozenset(
    {"consecutive_infrastructure_failures", "rate_limit", "relay_transient"}
)
FUSE_COOLDOWN_SECONDS = 600.0
# The relay pauses for concurrency are short; a full fuse cooldown would idle
# the freed slot far longer than the cap that caused it.
RELAY_COOLDOWN_SECONDS = 180.0
RELAY_COOLDOWN_REASONS = frozenset({"rate_limit", "relay_transient"})
# A per-key 403 that says the key's balance ran out, rather than a request-level
# or account-level rejection. The 2026-10-05b run stopped an entire wave after
# 1 accepted batch because one of three pool keys answered
# "token quota is not enough, token remain quota: 0.058035" on a proofs child:
# the other two keys served 21 batches normally in the same wave. An `auth`
# result is a property of the credential the child was bound to, not of the
# campaign, so it is worth resolving per key before giving up on the run.
KEY_EXHAUSTED_RE = re.compile(
    r"quota is not enough|insufficient[ _-]*(?:quota|balance|credit)|"
    r"余额不足|额度不足|invalid[ _-]*api[ _-]*key|unauthori[sz]ed|"
    # The provider answers a retired credential with "Invalid token", which none
    # of the wordings above match. Without this the batch is classified ``auth``
    # but no key is ever pruned, so the run halts with two healthy keys still in
    # the pool -- observed on the 2026-10-07 server test, where two of four keys
    # answered 401 and only the healthy pair could serve work.
    r"invalid[ _-]*token|authentication[ _-]*error",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
